docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:12:- Reconciliation verifies accepted PRD bytes and scope authorization, repository ownership and stable host prompt receipts. A supported live authoring attempt with correlated delivery retains its owner and remaining budget through recovery freeze; stopped/blocked owners stay retired. Unknown delivery evidence blocks repeated dispatch. Recovery remains observational until explicit resume.
docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:13:- Native PRD and issue authoring each allow three total attempts per approved stage, independent of issue/review budgets. Only accepted completion tools advance the stage. A settled turn with final prose, failed turn, or active-time timeout consumes an attempt; correctable completion validation stays in the live attempt.
docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:14:- Authoring attempts persist the owning session, prompt identity, draft root, captured remaining active-time budget and terminal result. Pending product/approval questions, direct native question tools and pause suspend the deadline. The injected planning clock supports deterministic deadline checks.
docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:15:- Failure retires tool authority before cancelling the recorded planning job. Retry requires confirmed cancellation and an idle/missing inspected owner; missing PRD interview sessions require intervention. PRD retries retain the same interview session; issue retries use reconciled fresh sessions. Each retry writes a separate draft namespace and receives accepted inputs, decision history, prior draft paths and the failure reason. Retired sessions retain their planning tool restrictions.
docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:16:- Exhaustion retains the blocker and native recovery request. Only a correlated real native “Reset budget and retry” answer grants another three attempts, with answer provenance recorded in `budgetResets`; “Stop” stops the run. Unavailable native input leaves the request pending and publishes its diagnostic. Stop/pause racing cancellation cannot allocate another attempt. Accepted completion atomically terminates the planning job, so its later turn settlement cannot cancel execution or consume another attempt. Authorized scope transitions retire superseded authoring jobs without aborting the continuing session; obsolete jobs cannot block final review.
docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:18:- `/ralph-resume` and `ralph_control` share the typed router resume service. Without an ID, it selects the latest feature ahead of unrelated setup records. `--base <commit>`/`selectedCommit` requests renewed explanation authorization; `--review`/`review` retains explicit review-only retry after terminal ownership release. Options cannot be combined, and unknown flags are rejected.
docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:19:- Native resume drives the saved phase; it does more than flip the control flag. Pending planning, failed baseline retry, scheduling, and automatic review continuation retain approvals and attempt budgets.
docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:20:- Pause rejects new jobs and suspends review scheduling while current jobs settle. Production executor submissions retain their attempt identity; mergers and successful in-flight checks wait at the next scheduling/ref boundary rather than becoming failed retries. Each resumed in-flight boundary rechecks the original epoch so stop followed by resume cannot revive a stale callback.
docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:26:- Review counters, attempt identities, approvals and artifact revisions are retained; controls do not reset budgets. Native resume preserves the stage owner and correlated unanswered questions.
docs/wiki/features/workflow-recovery.md:28:- An executing attempt with no submission/candidate can be recovered as a consumed failure only after a matching cancelled job, completed abort, idle/missing exact session and proven environment cleanup. Work and attempt history remain available to the next explicit retry. Busy, unknown or mismatched sessions stay blocked.
